SQL vs NoSQL

1. Core Difference
	SQL/Relational DB
		Data stored in tables, rows, columns
		Relationships through primary/foreign keys
		Uses SQL
		Strong schema and transaction support
		Good for complex relationships and changing query requirements
	NoSQL
		Umbrella term, not one database type
		Includes key-value, document, wide-column, graph, time-series, search, etc.
		Often designed around specific access patterns
		Usually easier to distribute horizontally for suitable workloads
		
2. SQL vs NoSQL
	| Feature       | SQL                            | NoSQL                            |
	| ------------- | ------------------------------ | -------------------------------- |
	| Data model    | Tables                         | Key-value/document/etc.          |
	| Schema        | Explicit/enforced              | Usually more flexible            |
	| Relationships | Joins + foreign keys           | Embed/duplicate/traverse         |
	| Transactions  | Strong general-purpose support | Varies by database               |
	| Queries       | Flexible                       | Often access-pattern driven      |
	| Scaling       | Vertical + replicas + sharding | Often horizontal-first           |
	| Best for      | Relational/transactional data  | High-scale/specialized workloads |


3. SQL Data Model
	Tables 
	Good when you need:
		Joins
		Foreign keys
		Constraints
		Multiple query patterns
		Strong transactional guarantees
		
4. NoSQL Models
	| Type            | Good For                              |
	| --------------- | ------------------------------------- |
	| **Key-Value**   | Sessions, caches, counters            |
	| **Document**    | Profiles, catalogs, content           |
	| **Wide-Column** | Huge write volumes, grouped lookups   |
	| **Graph**       | Relationships, recommendations, fraud |
	| **Time-Series** | Metrics, telemetry, IoT               |
	| **Search**      | Text search, filtering, ranking       |

5. Schema
	The statement "SQL has schema, NoSQL has no schema" is misleading.
	in sql - database enforces the structure
	nosql - Records may have different fields: But the application still expects some schema. It may simply be enforced through application code, validation, API contracts, or document versions instead of entirely by the database.
	
6. Query Patterns
	sql - Good when you don't know every future query. You can query relationships using joins.
	NoSQL - Good when access patterns are known beforehand. You design the data around how it will be read.
	
7. Transactions & Consistency
	SQL is usually the safer default when multiple records must change atomically.
	e.g - banck transfer, A-=300 , B+=300
	Both operations need to succeed together.
	NoSQL transaction support varies by database:
		Some provide atomic operations on one key
		Some support transactions across multiple documents
		Some provide conditional writes
		Distributed databases may have limitations across partitions
	So don't simply ask: "Does NoSQL support transactions?"
	Ask: "What exactly can the transaction guarantee?"

8. Scaling
	SQL   → vertical scaling
	NoSQL → horizontal scaling
	SQL can scale using:
		Bigger machines
		Read replicas
		Partitioning
		Sharding
		Connection pooling
		Caching
		Distributed SQL
		
	NoSQL can make horizontal scaling easier for workloads designed around partition keys, but it isn't magic. Bad partition keys, hot keys, huge documents, and cross-partition queries can still ruin your afternoon.
